From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 02:30:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(web): fetch scroll-up history ahead of its landing, in
 larger pages

Scrolling up through a long transcript stuttered, worst on a phone: every
20 items cost a round trip that only started once the pane was still, then
a prepend hitch, so a reader paging back through a few hundred items hit
the top and waited sixteen times over.

The history loader now fetches the next older page as soon as a seeking
gesture brings the pane within the load threshold, ahead of the landing
decision that may wait for a fling to settle; `loadMoreHistory` then lands
the prefetched page from memory. One page per cursor is ever in flight, a
failed prefetch is forgotten so the reader's own request retries it, and
the page is dropped with its conversation.

Scroll-up pages grow from 20 items to 100, or 60 on a mobile-width
viewport (the shell's `useIsMobileViewport` breakpoint); only the rows in
view mount, so a page costs one request plus one prepend whatever its
size. The per-gesture chaining budget is kept as 600 items rather than 30
pages, so a folded tool-heavy turn still cannot page the whole transcript
in with the reader's hands off. The reconnect backfill keeps its 20-item
page.

On a 420-item session at a phone viewport with a 100 ms RTT and a 4x CPU
slowdown: 16 page fetches become 6, time stalled at the top waiting for a
page drops from 2.5 s to 0.5 s, and long tasks during the scroll fall from
6.0 s to 2.5 s. Desktop goes from 16 fetches to 4.

Not upstream yet: proposed from fix/history-prefetch; drop this copy once it merges.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
(cherry picked from commit efa0347afede58d67739c9b34c22a5d9b2542476)
Docs: none (performance: older history loads ahead of the scroll, in larger pages; no setting)
